# Perceived performance

> A fast product responds immediately. A premium product also explains intelligently when the result cannot be immediate.

## Every asynchronous interaction must answer

1. Did the system receive the action?
2. What is happening?
3. How far has it progressed (when knowable)?
4. Can the user continue working?
5. What happens if it fails?
6. Can it be retried safely?

## Required practices

- Immediate acknowledgment of interactions (visual/state change < 100ms perceived)
- Optimistic updates where safe; clear processing where not
- Stable skeleton geometry matching final layout
- Progressive rendering (show shell, then content)
- Route prefetching for likely next navigation
- Intentional lazy loading (never lazy-load LCP element)
- Image dimension reservation (width/height or aspect-ratio)
- Avoid unnecessary blocking requests on critical path
- Avoid serial data waterfalls where parallel fetch is safe
- Preserve user input during failures
- Background refresh without unnecessary UI disruption
- Caching appropriate to data freshness requirements
- Reduced animation on `prefers-reduced-motion`
- No large decorative asset delaying primary experience

## Performance budget

Targets and measurements live in `.cursor/experience-memory/performance-budget.md`.

Do not invent measured results. Set targets; record measurements after audit via `perceived-performance-engineer`.

Align Core Web Vitals with `pixel-perfect-quality.mdc`:

| Metric | Target |
| --- | --- |
| LCP | ≤ 2.5s |
| INP | ≤ 200ms |
| CLS | ≤ 0.1 |

## Invoke

- `perceived-performance-engineer` when adding async workflows, data fetching, uploads, AI generation, or heavy routes
- `layout-perfection-critic` for CWV measurement on changed public pages

Update `performance-budget.md` when budgets change.
